    ($SATQ$f4fd123c$SATQ$, $SATQ$3ffb108e-969f-4e8b-a181-2d502ec28aef$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$85c1b9e8-f03c-4220-875e-cc4b81948e47$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The African Games Co-production Market, one of over 180 annual international conferences supporting video game development, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the growth of the African gaming industry by helping start-up studios in Africa find partners.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"bfc6eb03-3d2b-4029-9d1b-bd1b4deb4a3d","content":"<p>promote</p>"},{"id":"52b280b1-d86c-44e3-a920-1ee82c20f1f9","content":"<p>are promoting</p>"},{"id":"24a3f485-d722-4ed5-b1c0-dbf093b8dc7a","content":"<p>promotes</p>"},{"id":"600106f3-dba2-448a-a499-d7f97ba28093","content":"<p>have promoted</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["24a3f485-d722-4ed5-b1c0-dbf093b8dc7a"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The subject of the sentence is &ldquo;The African Games Co-production Market.&rdquo; That&rsquo;s one market, so it&rsquo;s a singular noun, which means it needs a singular verb. &ldquo;Promotes&rdquo; is the only singular verb among the choices.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice creates a subject-verb agreement error. The subject &ldquo;The African Games Co-production Market&rdquo; is singular, but the verb &ldquo;promote&rdquo; is plural. Choice B is incorrect. This choice creates a subject-verb agreement error. The subject &ldquo;The African Games Co-production Market&rdquo; is singular, but the verb &ldquo;are promoting&rdquo; is plural. Choice D is incorrect. This choice creates a subject-verb agreement error. The subject &ldquo;The African Games Co-production Market&rdquo; is singular, but the verb &ldquo;have promoted&rdquo; is plural. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-FSS-M002$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$af6e212c-367f-4cb3-83bf-9cf79d1fa39f$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"f4fd123c","external_id":"3ffb108e-969f-4e8b-a181-2d502ec28aef","vaultid":"85c1b9e8-f03c-4220-875e-cc4b81948e47","uId":"5530c010-eae9-45e7-a876-1256bdb36eff","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The African Games Co-production Market, one of over 180 annual international conferences supporting video game development, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the growth of the African gaming industry by helping start-up studios in Africa find partners.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"bfc6eb03-3d2b-4029-9d1b-bd1b4deb4a3d","content":"<p>promote</p>"},{"id":"52b280b1-d86c-44e3-a920-1ee82c20f1f9","content":"<p>are promoting</p>"},{"id":"24a3f485-d722-4ed5-b1c0-dbf093b8dc7a","content":"<p>promotes</p>"},{"id":"600106f3-dba2-448a-a499-d7f97ba28093","content":"<p>have promoted</p>"}],"keys":["24a3f485-d722-4ed5-b1c0-dbf093b8dc7a"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The subject of the sentence is &ldquo;The African Games Co-production Market.&rdquo; That&rsquo;s one market, so it&rsquo;s a singular noun, which means it needs a singular verb. &ldquo;Promotes&rdquo; is the only singular verb among the choices.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. This choice creates a subject-verb agreement error. The subject &ldquo;The African Games Co-production Market&rdquo; is singular, but the verb &ldquo;promote&rdquo; is plural. Choice B is incorrect. This choice creates a subject-verb agreement error. The subject &ldquo;The African Games Co-production Market&rdquo; is singular, but the verb &ldquo;are promoting&rdquo; is plural. Choice D is incorrect. This choice creates a subject-verb agreement error. The subject &ldquo;The African Games Co-production Market&rdquo; is singular, but the verb &ldquo;have promoted&rdquo; is plural. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-FSS-M002","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"af6e212c-367f-4cb3-83bf-9cf79d1fa39f","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959367,"updateDate":1691007959367}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959367, 1691007959367),
    ($SATQ$f768090a$SATQ$, $SATQ$58d56d87-b826-4444-a46e-ca0aa7fd474e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Walt Whitman&rsquo;s <em>Leaves of Grass</em> first appeared in <span data-ssml-say-as="date">1855</span> as a slim collection of twelve poems, but Whitman would revise and expand it substantially over the next four decades. These extensive <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the addition of hundreds of new poems, the removal of some existing ones, and the insertion of prefatory material, reflected the poet&rsquo;s evolving literary perspective and experience of the US Civil War.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"7478be2d-486d-4ba7-b47c-6349520135b2","content":"<p>changes<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> including</p>"},{"id":"8f4d5146-874e-4901-81f2-feebd692f32f","content":"<p>changes would include</p>"},{"id":"c8a58429-c58f-4ed5-9007-241fa4bec5d7","content":"<p>changes included</p>"},{"id":"2141b878-d47c-48a8-94e8-b2dd16e1d88b","content":"<p>changes<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> include</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["7478be2d-486d-4ba7-b47c-6349520135b2"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verb forms within a sentence. The nonfinite present participle "including" is correctly used to form a supplementary element that interrupts the main clause "These extensive changes&hellip;reflected the poet&rsquo;s evolving literary perspective and experience of the US Civil War." This supplementary element, offset by commas after "changes" and "material," provides examples of the changes Whitman made to <em>Leaves of Grass</em>.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The finite modal verb "would include" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to form a supplementary element within the main clause. Choice C is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The finite past tense verb "included" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to form a supplementary element within the main clause. Choice D is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The finite present tense verb "include" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to form a supplementary element within the main clause.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"f768090a","external_id":"58d56d87-b826-4444-a46e-ca0aa7fd474e","vaultid":null,"uId":"7d18b164-9683-445e-a3eb-35f8b14a0717","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Walt Whitman&rsquo;s <em>Leaves of Grass</em> first appeared in <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1855</span> as a slim collection of twelve poems, but Whitman would revise and expand it substantially over the next four decades. These extensive <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the addition of hundreds of new poems, the removal of some existing ones, and the insertion of prefatory material, reflected the poet&rsquo;s evolving literary perspective and experience of the US Civil War.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"7478be2d-486d-4ba7-b47c-6349520135b2","content":"<p>changes<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> including</p>"},{"id":"8f4d5146-874e-4901-81f2-feebd692f32f","content":"<p>changes would include</p>"},{"id":"c8a58429-c58f-4ed5-9007-241fa4bec5d7","content":"<p>changes included</p>"},{"id":"2141b878-d47c-48a8-94e8-b2dd16e1d88b","content":"<p>changes<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> include</p>"}],"keys":["7478be2d-486d-4ba7-b47c-6349520135b2"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of verb forms within a sentence. The nonfinite present participle \"including\" is correctly used to form a supplementary element that interrupts the main clause \"These extensive changes&hellip;reflected the poet&rsquo;s evolving literary perspective and experience of the US Civil War.\" This supplementary element, offset by commas after \"changes\" and \"material,\" provides examples of the changes Whitman made to <em>Leaves of Grass</em>.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The finite modal verb \"would include\" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to form a supplementary element within the main clause. Choice C is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The finite past tense verb \"included\" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to form a supplementary element within the main clause. Choice D is incorrect because it results in an ungrammatical sentence. The finite present tense verb \"include\" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to form a supplementary element within the main clause.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669207,"updateDate":1743430669207}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669207, 1743430669207),
    ($SATQ$f78997cf$SATQ$, $SATQ$0f97b9c6-806b-4be8-b57f-75c74183ed6a$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Recent analysis of 32532 Thereus&mdash;an outer solar system object orbiting the Sun between Jupiter and Saturn&mdash;has determined its color to be gray, suggesting an icy composition. Such interpretations are ultimately <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the object&rsquo;s gray coloration may be an incidental effect of radiation, solar wind, or collisions with other objects rather than evidence of its physical makeup.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"16616896-46ee-4001-87d0-e6b3a4f79f30","content":"<p>speculative<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> though&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"2b9eaecd-7fc0-4edf-8273-bfe9a00e277c","content":"<p>speculative<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> though<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span>&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"45df71a7-59f1-4b32-bd96-3cfec0d4bae4","content":"<p>speculative<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span> though&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"19fda8b9-2c69-49b5-ac0c-3b738701cfd1","content":"<p>speculative<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> though<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span>&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["2b9eaecd-7fc0-4edf-8273-bfe9a00e277c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation in a sentence. This choice correctly uses a comma to separate the supplementary adverb "though" from the preceding main clause ("Such interpretations are ultimately speculative") and uses a semicolon to join the next main clause ("the object&rsquo;s...makeup") to the rest of the sentence. Further, placing the semicolon after "though" indicates that the information in the preceding main clause (interpretations of an outer solar system object&rsquo;s composition based on its color are ultimately speculative) is a qualification of the information in the previous sentence (the composition of an outer solar system object is suggested by its color).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it fails to mark the boundary between the two main clauses with appropriate punctuation. Choice C is incorrect because placing the semicolon after "speculative" illogically indicates that the information in the next main clause ("the object&rsquo;s...makeup") is a qualification of the information in the previous clause ("Such...speculative"). Choice D is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Commas can&rsquo;t be used in this way to punctuate a supplementary word or phrase between two main clauses.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"f78997cf","external_id":"0f97b9c6-806b-4be8-b57f-75c74183ed6a","vaultid":null,"uId":"16719621-4e36-4fcf-b7e2-b8d422aa3aaa","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Recent analysis of 32532 Thereus&mdash;an outer solar system object orbiting the Sun between Jupiter and Saturn&mdash;has determined its color to be gray, suggesting an icy composition. Such interpretations are ultimately <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the object&rsquo;s gray coloration may be an incidental effect of radiation, solar wind, or collisions with other objects rather than evidence of its physical makeup.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"16616896-46ee-4001-87d0-e6b3a4f79f30","content":"<p>speculative<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> though&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"2b9eaecd-7fc0-4edf-8273-bfe9a00e277c","content":"<p>speculative<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> though<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span>&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"45df71a7-59f1-4b32-bd96-3cfec0d4bae4","content":"<p>speculative<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span> though&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"19fda8b9-2c69-49b5-ac0c-3b738701cfd1","content":"<p>speculative<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> though<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span>&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["2b9eaecd-7fc0-4edf-8273-bfe9a00e277c"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation in a sentence. This choice correctly uses a comma to separate the supplementary adverb \"though\" from the preceding main clause (\"Such interpretations are ultimately speculative\") and uses a semicolon to join the next main clause (\"the object&rsquo;s...makeup\") to the rest of the sentence. Further, placing the semicolon after \"though\" indicates that the information in the preceding main clause (interpretations of an outer solar system object&rsquo;s composition based on its color are ultimately speculative) is a qualification of the information in the previous sentence (the composition of an outer solar system object is suggested by its color).</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it fails to mark the boundary between the two main clauses with appropriate punctuation. Choice C is incorrect because placing the semicolon after \"speculative\" illogically indicates that the information in the next main clause (\"the object&rsquo;s...makeup\") is a qualification of the information in the previous clause (\"Such...speculative\"). Choice D is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. Commas can&rsquo;t be used in this way to punctuate a supplementary word or phrase between two main clauses.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061175,"updateDate":1755117061175}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061175, 1755117061175),
    ($SATQ$f868d438$SATQ$, $SATQ$af8c6ab9-7395-46cf-8f90-531ef0739c5d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$bfce9a3d-c7b1-4f47-98cd-207e9e9afeb4$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In 2018, the innovative works of Congolese sculptor and architect Bodys Isek <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> were featured in <em>City Dreams</em>, a solo exhibition at New York&rsquo;s Museum of Modern Art.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"9059cff6-e9a7-4f91-a196-ad29e1010cb6","content":"<p>Kingelez;</p>"},{"id":"da326b9e-cba3-4fa0-934f-295500cc50a9","content":"<p>Kingelez,</p>"},{"id":"9784353f-4a61-4c7f-970f-b77f646b66a0","content":"<p>Kingelez</p>"},{"id":"c6ccb0cf-d1d9-4fb1-8bf6-2d2ee067e211","content":"<p>Kingelez:</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["9784353f-4a61-4c7f-970f-b77f646b66a0"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation between a subject and a verb. No punctuation is needed when the subject of a sentence is immediately followed by a main verb. In this case, the sentence&rsquo;s subject (&ldquo;the innovative works of Congolese sculptor and architect Bodys Isek Kingelez&rdquo;) is followed by the main verb &ldquo;were featured,&rdquo; and no punctuation should come between them. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-745-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$ce039395-7563-4515-bbac-f046e15485f4$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"f868d438","external_id":"af8c6ab9-7395-46cf-8f90-531ef0739c5d","vaultid":"bfce9a3d-c7b1-4f47-98cd-207e9e9afeb4","uId":"d3ea74dc-4d86-435c-9e98-41c7d6d6af33","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In 2018, the innovative works of Congolese sculptor and architect Bodys Isek <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> were featured in <em>City Dreams</em>, a solo exhibition at New York&rsquo;s Museum of Modern Art.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"9059cff6-e9a7-4f91-a196-ad29e1010cb6","content":"<p>Kingelez;</p>"},{"id":"da326b9e-cba3-4fa0-934f-295500cc50a9","content":"<p>Kingelez,</p>"},{"id":"9784353f-4a61-4c7f-970f-b77f646b66a0","content":"<p>Kingelez</p>"},{"id":"c6ccb0cf-d1d9-4fb1-8bf6-2d2ee067e211","content":"<p>Kingelez:</p>"}],"keys":["9784353f-4a61-4c7f-970f-b77f646b66a0"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation between a subject and a verb. No punctuation is needed when the subject of a sentence is immediately followed by a main verb. In this case, the sentence&rsquo;s subject (&ldquo;the innovative works of Congolese sculptor and architect Bodys Isek Kingelez&rdquo;) is followed by the main verb &ldquo;were featured,&rdquo; and no punctuation should come between them. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb.&nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the subject and the verb. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-745-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"ce039395-7563-4515-bbac-f046e15485f4","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959350,"updateDate":1691007959350}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959350, 1691007959350),
    ($SATQ$fa07bcb6$SATQ$, $SATQ$a6fec74b-b685-46bf-b416-80f047cab9f3$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Ganga is a folk singing tradition that originated in the Dinaric Alps in southern Europe. Ganga singers sing different melodies at the same time. The clashing notes can echo a long way across the mountains, which is why <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"1ca3f7b0-6972-4f63-bfed-5368b1183742","content":"<p>ganga has been used as a communication method<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">?</span></p>"},{"id":"79fb4285-d6e3-4c9e-aee4-cbfed34463ab","content":"<p>ganga has been used as a communication method<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">.</span></p>"},{"id":"cdfee1e7-f242-47b2-8d61-203402800b8d","content":"<p>has ganga been used as a communication method<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">.</span></p>"},{"id":"79cc5d54-1c15-4bfb-a978-5bb106b5d514","content":"<p>has ganga been used as a communication method<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">?</span></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["79fb4285-d6e3-4c9e-aee4-cbfed34463ab"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is end-of-sentence punctuation. This choice correctly uses a period to punctuate a declarative sentence ending with a relative clause that presents an indirect question (&ldquo;why ganga has been used as a communication method.&rdquo;)</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. It&rsquo;s unconventional to use a question mark to punctuate a declarative sentence that presents an indirect question. Choice C is incorrect. The structure requires a declarative clause at the end of the sentence, not an interrogative clause that asks a direct question, such as &ldquo;has ganga been used as a communication method.&rdquo; Choice D is incorrect. The structure requires a declarative clause at the end of the sentence, not an interrogative clause that asks a direct question, such as &ldquo;has ganga been used as a communication method.&rdquo;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"fa07bcb6","external_id":"a6fec74b-b685-46bf-b416-80f047cab9f3","vaultid":null,"uId":"ccef6295-1ec9-4184-8e12-37bc488d3e80","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Ganga is a folk singing tradition that originated in the Dinaric Alps in southern Europe. Ganga singers sing different melodies at the same time. The clashing notes can echo a long way across the mountains, which is why <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"1ca3f7b0-6972-4f63-bfed-5368b1183742","content":"<p>ganga has been used as a communication method<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">?</span></p>"},{"id":"79fb4285-d6e3-4c9e-aee4-cbfed34463ab","content":"<p>ganga has been used as a communication method<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">.</span></p>"},{"id":"cdfee1e7-f242-47b2-8d61-203402800b8d","content":"<p>has ganga been used as a communication method<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">.</span></p>"},{"id":"79cc5d54-1c15-4bfb-a978-5bb106b5d514","content":"<p>has ganga been used as a communication method<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">?</span></p>"}],"keys":["79fb4285-d6e3-4c9e-aee4-cbfed34463ab"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is end-of-sentence punctuation. This choice correctly uses a period to punctuate a declarative sentence ending with a relative clause that presents an indirect question (&ldquo;why ganga has been used as a communication method.&rdquo;)</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. It&rsquo;s unconventional to use a question mark to punctuate a declarative sentence that presents an indirect question. Choice C is incorrect. The structure requires a declarative clause at the end of the sentence, not an interrogative clause that asks a direct question, such as &ldquo;has ganga been used as a communication method.&rdquo; Choice D is incorrect. The structure requires a declarative clause at the end of the sentence, not an interrogative clause that asks a direct question, such as &ldquo;has ganga been used as a communication method.&rdquo;</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771170,"updateDate":1776362771170}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771170, 1776362771170),
    ($SATQ$fa36d803$SATQ$, $SATQ$849dbfd4-a703-4e5f-9a77-444840ef8712$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Long attributed to Jacques-Louis David, the preeminent Neoclassical painter of his day, the 1801 painting <em>Marie Jos&eacute;phine Charlotte du Val d&rsquo;Ognes</em> gained fresh attention in the 1990s when art historians discovered that the painting&mdash;which depicts a solitary young woman sketching&mdash;was actually the work of little-known French portrait <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> Marie-Denise Villers (1774&ndash;1821).</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"126de9f6-175b-4873-94f5-5d1bbaae76fd","content":"<p>artist&mdash;</p>"},{"id":"e9c1ff1f-7856-47c6-83e2-0afc8b1301a0","content":"<p>artist</p>"},{"id":"46bf9b96-2612-4e21-af08-320077936119","content":"<p>artist:</p>"},{"id":"de470a9d-d1cf-4678-a241-996c47fee740","content":"<p>artist,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["e9c1ff1f-7856-47c6-83e2-0afc8b1301a0"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation between titles and proper nouns. No punctuation is needed to set off the proper noun "Marie-Denise Villers" from the title that describes Villers, "little-known French portrait artist."</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because no punctuation is needed. Choice C is incorrect because no punctuation is needed. Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"fa36d803","external_id":"849dbfd4-a703-4e5f-9a77-444840ef8712","vaultid":null,"uId":"917fd6b4-a2ee-4e5d-b7bb-1c7e93370512","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Long attributed to Jacques-Louis David, the preeminent Neoclassical painter of his day, the 1801 painting <em>Marie Jos&eacute;phine Charlotte du Val d&rsquo;Ognes</em> gained fresh attention in the 1990s when art historians discovered that the painting&mdash;which depicts a solitary young woman sketching&mdash;was actually the work of little-known French portrait <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> Marie-Denise Villers (1774&ndash;1821).</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"126de9f6-175b-4873-94f5-5d1bbaae76fd","content":"<p>artist&mdash;</p>"},{"id":"e9c1ff1f-7856-47c6-83e2-0afc8b1301a0","content":"<p>artist</p>"},{"id":"46bf9b96-2612-4e21-af08-320077936119","content":"<p>artist:</p>"},{"id":"de470a9d-d1cf-4678-a241-996c47fee740","content":"<p>artist,</p>"}],"keys":["e9c1ff1f-7856-47c6-83e2-0afc8b1301a0"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation between titles and proper nouns. No punctuation is needed to set off the proper noun \"Marie-Denise Villers\" from the title that describes Villers, \"little-known French portrait artist.\"</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because no punctuation is needed. Choice C is incorrect because no punctuation is needed. Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323049,"updateDate":1730147323049}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323049, 1730147323049),
    ($SATQ$fba5d8d1$SATQ$, $SATQ$5bbed69a-9680-4d41-95a7-8291a97873e4$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$7c5e9531-cae1-4514-8a0c-90da0e988f88$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In a 2016 study, Eastern Washington University psychologist Amani El-Alayli found that, among the study participants who experienced frisson (a physiological response akin to goosebumps or getting the chills) while listening to music, there was one personality trait that they scored particularly <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> openness to experience.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"10d09c25-e2f3-4deb-82cc-89cf13222150","content":"<p>high. On</p>"},{"id":"c47abf1a-6db6-402c-a329-20f276b2e7d9","content":"<p>high on;</p>"},{"id":"a6f9b08b-d266-4979-ba94-4ca4aabaa4b1","content":"<p>high on</p>"},{"id":"93ad7dca-228b-4c5b-a15a-1d980793cf1d","content":"<p>high on:</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["93ad7dca-228b-4c5b-a15a-1d980793cf1d"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between a main clause and a supplementary phrase. In this choice, a colon is correctly used to mark the boundary between the main clause ("there...on") and the supplementary phrase ("openness to experience") and to introduce the information that identifies which personality trait participants scored especially high on.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a rhetorically unacceptable sentence fragment beginning with "on" and separates a necessary preposition from the clause beginning with "there." Choice B is incorrect because a semicolon can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join the main clause ("there...on") and the supplementary phrase ("openness to experience"). A semicolon is conventionally used to join two main clauses, whereas a colon is conventionally used to introduce an element that explains or amplifies the information in the preceding clause, making the colon the better choice in this context. Choice C is incorrect because it fails to mark the boundary between the main clause ("there...on") and the supplementary phrase ("openness to experience").</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-BOU-H003$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$26c7def4-1368-4717-8d73-ac64f401350b$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"fba5d8d1","external_id":"5bbed69a-9680-4d41-95a7-8291a97873e4","vaultid":"7c5e9531-cae1-4514-8a0c-90da0e988f88","uId":"2d720381-2410-4086-91b8-05dd4e8dcdeb","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In a 2016 study, Eastern Washington University psychologist Amani El-Alayli found that, among the study participants who experienced frisson (a physiological response akin to goosebumps or getting the chills) while listening to music, there was one personality trait that they scored particularly <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> openness to experience.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"10d09c25-e2f3-4deb-82cc-89cf13222150","content":"<p>high. On</p>"},{"id":"c47abf1a-6db6-402c-a329-20f276b2e7d9","content":"<p>high on;</p>"},{"id":"a6f9b08b-d266-4979-ba94-4ca4aabaa4b1","content":"<p>high on</p>"},{"id":"93ad7dca-228b-4c5b-a15a-1d980793cf1d","content":"<p>high on:</p>"}],"keys":["93ad7dca-228b-4c5b-a15a-1d980793cf1d"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between a main clause and a supplementary phrase. In this choice, a colon is correctly used to mark the boundary between the main clause (\"there...on\") and the supplementary phrase (\"openness to experience\") and to introduce the information that identifies which personality trait participants scored especially high on.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a rhetorically unacceptable sentence fragment beginning with \"on\" and separates a necessary preposition from the clause beginning with \"there.\" Choice B is incorrect because a semicolon can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join the main clause (\"there...on\") and the supplementary phrase (\"openness to experience\"). A semicolon is conventionally used to join two main clauses, whereas a colon is conventionally used to introduce an element that explains or amplifies the information in the preceding clause, making the colon the better choice in this context. Choice C is incorrect because it fails to mark the boundary between the main clause (\"there...on\") and the supplementary phrase (\"openness to experience\").</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-BOU-H003","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"26c7def4-1368-4717-8d73-ac64f401350b","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959358,"updateDate":1691007959358}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959358, 1691007959358),
    ($SATQ$fcab3630$SATQ$, $SATQ$d99f186d-1906-41f6-a1df-00cd641b8ca2$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In his <em>Naturalis historia</em>, Pliny the Elder praised Hipparchus&rsquo;s star catalog, a second-century BCE list of roughly 850 different stars&rsquo; celestial positions. For centuries, scholars dreamed about locating a copy of this legendary lost <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> fantasy (partially) became reality in 2022, when researchers uncovered traces of the star catalog on a palimpsest, a reused parchment.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"3922e76b-4d9c-4403-a71c-f61e9241dc0f","content":"<p>work<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> that</p>"},{"id":"c7a5c91f-0d41-4df3-b447-c3208be07bad","content":"<p>work that</p>"},{"id":"9326969d-38bb-494d-952b-40db9e03af7c","content":"<p>work<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">.</span> That</p>"},{"id":"12708730-0499-445e-88ed-0a78b98a4bd3","content":"<p>work and that</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["9326969d-38bb-494d-952b-40db9e03af7c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between sentences. In this choice, the period is used correctly to mark the boundary between one sentence ("For&hellip;work") and another ("That&hellip;parchment").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between sentences. Choice B is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The sentences ("For&hellip;work" and "that&hellip;parchment") are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. Choice D is incorrect. Without a comma preceding it, the conjunction "and" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join sentences.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"fcab3630","external_id":"d99f186d-1906-41f6-a1df-00cd641b8ca2","vaultid":null,"uId":"83871681-d628-4d70-97a5-20feb38b9ff9","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In his <em>Naturalis historia</em>, Pliny the Elder praised Hipparchus&rsquo;s star catalog, a second-century BCE list of roughly 850 different stars&rsquo; celestial positions. For centuries, scholars dreamed about locating a copy of this legendary lost <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> fantasy (partially) became reality in 2022, when researchers uncovered traces of the star catalog on a palimpsest, a reused parchment.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"3922e76b-4d9c-4403-a71c-f61e9241dc0f","content":"<p>work<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> that</p>"},{"id":"c7a5c91f-0d41-4df3-b447-c3208be07bad","content":"<p>work that</p>"},{"id":"9326969d-38bb-494d-952b-40db9e03af7c","content":"<p>work<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">.</span> That</p>"},{"id":"12708730-0499-445e-88ed-0a78b98a4bd3","content":"<p>work and that</p>"}],"keys":["9326969d-38bb-494d-952b-40db9e03af7c"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between sentences. In this choice, the period is used correctly to mark the boundary between one sentence (\"For&hellip;work\") and another (\"That&hellip;parchment\").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between sentences. Choice B is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The sentences (\"For&hellip;work\" and \"that&hellip;parchment\") are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. Choice D is incorrect. Without a comma preceding it, the conjunction \"and\" can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join sentences.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669213,"updateDate":1743430669213}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669213, 1743430669213),
    ($SATQ$fcaff694$SATQ$, $SATQ$ac4962dd-95b7-4b55-a625-8e38af6f3f37$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$d3c8559f-29cc-4f61-b9b9-9477f4cfa0c9$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The city of Pompeii, which was buried in ash following the eruption of Mount Vesuvius in 79 CE, continues to be studied by archaeologists. Unfortunately, as <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> attest, archaeological excavations have disrupted ash deposits at the site, causing valuable information about the eruption to be lost. &nbsp; &nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4998f609-9805-4740-ba12-07dbf2fbd647","content":"<p>researchers, Roberto Scandone and Christopher Kilburn,</p>"},{"id":"2ee5d643-fa2d-4e78-9ec3-21516872ebc2","content":"<p>researchers, Roberto Scandone and Christopher Kilburn</p>"},{"id":"28f918f4-38bd-4062-bc9e-27d9ade24d93","content":"<p>researchers Roberto Scandone and Christopher Kilburn</p>"},{"id":"a8b10737-142e-47ac-89b0-ea53c09d59a2","content":"<p>researchers Roberto Scandone, and Christopher Kilburn</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["28f918f4-38bd-4062-bc9e-27d9ade24d93"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the punctuation of a restrictive coordinated noun phrase. No punctuation is needed within or around the coordinated noun phrase &ldquo;researchers Roberto Scandone and Christopher Kilburn&rdquo; because it would create an illogical separation between the noun &ldquo;researchers&rdquo; and the coordinated noun phrase &ldquo;Roberto Scandone and Christopher Kilburn.&rdquo; </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because no punctuation is needed. Placing a pair of commas around the coordinated noun phrase &ldquo;Roberto Scandone and Christopher Kilburn&rdquo; creates an illogical separation between the noun &ldquo;researchers&rdquo; and the aforementioned coordinated noun phrase. In this case, it illogically suggests that researchers in general bear the specific names Roberto Scandone and Christopher Kilburn. Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the noun &ldquo;researchers&rdquo; and the coordinated noun phrase &ldquo;Roberto Scandone and Christopher Kilburn.&rdquo; Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed within the coordinated noun phrase &ldquo;Roberto Scandone and Christopher Kilburn.&rdquo; </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-652-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$cca44d3b-1c19-4c32-986f-62bedbd4d9ee$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"fcaff694","external_id":"ac4962dd-95b7-4b55-a625-8e38af6f3f37","vaultid":"d3c8559f-29cc-4f61-b9b9-9477f4cfa0c9","uId":"627b15dc-7b1a-4d1a-8e29-11c954ce3216","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The city of Pompeii, which was buried in ash following the eruption of Mount Vesuvius in 79 CE, continues to be studied by archaeologists. Unfortunately, as <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> attest, archaeological excavations have disrupted ash deposits at the site, causing valuable information about the eruption to be lost. &nbsp; &nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4998f609-9805-4740-ba12-07dbf2fbd647","content":"<p>researchers, Roberto Scandone and Christopher Kilburn,</p>"},{"id":"2ee5d643-fa2d-4e78-9ec3-21516872ebc2","content":"<p>researchers, Roberto Scandone and Christopher Kilburn</p>"},{"id":"28f918f4-38bd-4062-bc9e-27d9ade24d93","content":"<p>researchers Roberto Scandone and Christopher Kilburn</p>"},{"id":"a8b10737-142e-47ac-89b0-ea53c09d59a2","content":"<p>researchers Roberto Scandone, and Christopher Kilburn</p>"}],"keys":["28f918f4-38bd-4062-bc9e-27d9ade24d93"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The convention being tested is the punctuation of a restrictive coordinated noun phrase. No punctuation is needed within or around the coordinated noun phrase &ldquo;researchers Roberto Scandone and Christopher Kilburn&rdquo; because it would create an illogical separation between the noun &ldquo;researchers&rdquo; and the coordinated noun phrase &ldquo;Roberto Scandone and Christopher Kilburn.&rdquo; </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because no punctuation is needed. Placing a pair of commas around the coordinated noun phrase &ldquo;Roberto Scandone and Christopher Kilburn&rdquo; creates an illogical separation between the noun &ldquo;researchers&rdquo; and the aforementioned coordinated noun phrase. In this case, it illogically suggests that researchers in general bear the specific names Roberto Scandone and Christopher Kilburn. Choice B is incorrect because no punctuation is needed between the noun &ldquo;researchers&rdquo; and the coordinated noun phrase &ldquo;Roberto Scandone and Christopher Kilburn.&rdquo; Choice D is incorrect because no punctuation is needed within the coordinated noun phrase &ldquo;Roberto Scandone and Christopher Kilburn.&rdquo; </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-652-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"cca44d3b-1c19-4c32-986f-62bedbd4d9ee","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959341,"updateDate":1691007959341}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959341, 1691007959341),
    ($SATQ$fced396a$SATQ$, $SATQ$fa95da1d-7829-4ac7-9367-cc29aa6fd8c8$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The Herfindahl-Hirschman Index (HHI), a commonly used measure of competition between companies in a particular market, ranges from a score of zero to 10,000 points. Compared with that of a highly concentrated market&mdash;that is, a market controlled by very few companies&mdash;<span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"78604a33-4240-4472-b697-bfc16a4bd487","content":"<p>a market that is less concentrated will have a much lower HHI score.</p>"},{"id":"86ab968e-86d8-4ab1-bab6-67ad1351500c","content":"<p>the HHI score of a less concentrated market will be much lower.</p>"},{"id":"18d5ba28-4c03-4f66-b427-e9aa701bf4b7","content":"<p>when a market is less concentrated, its HHI score will be much lower.</p>"},{"id":"134c8154-ce38-4174-bbdb-a77c5989a57d","content":"<p>a less concentrated market will have an HHI score that is much lower.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["86ab968e-86d8-4ab1-bab6-67ad1351500c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes the noun phrase "the HHI...market" the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrase "compared...market." In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that the HHI score of a less concentrated market&mdash;and not another noun in the sentence&mdash;is being compared with the HHI score of a highly concentrated market.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of "a market that is less concentrated" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that a market is being compared with an HHI score. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a confusing, illogical sentence. The placement of the modifier "when a market is less concentrated" after "a highly concentrated market" is contradictory. Furthermore, it&rsquo;s ambiguous what "its" is referring to in "its HHI score," resulting in an illogical comparison. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of "a less concentrated market" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that a market is being compared with an HHI score.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"fced396a","external_id":"fa95da1d-7829-4ac7-9367-cc29aa6fd8c8","vaultid":null,"uId":"1b5de43a-f9a8-45c9-bdee-23c593b4284f","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>The Herfindahl-Hirschman Index (HHI), a commonly used measure of competition between companies in a particular market, ranges from a score of zero to 10,000 points. Compared with that of a highly concentrated market&mdash;that is, a market controlled by very few companies&mdash;<span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"78604a33-4240-4472-b697-bfc16a4bd487","content":"<p>a market that is less concentrated will have a much lower HHI score.</p>"},{"id":"86ab968e-86d8-4ab1-bab6-67ad1351500c","content":"<p>the HHI score of a less concentrated market will be much lower.</p>"},{"id":"18d5ba28-4c03-4f66-b427-e9aa701bf4b7","content":"<p>when a market is less concentrated, its HHI score will be much lower.</p>"},{"id":"134c8154-ce38-4174-bbdb-a77c5989a57d","content":"<p>a less concentrated market will have an HHI score that is much lower.</p>"}],"keys":["86ab968e-86d8-4ab1-bab6-67ad1351500c"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-modifier placement. This choice makes the noun phrase \"the HHI...market\" the subject of the sentence and places it immediately after the modifying phrase \"compared...market.\" In doing so, this choice clearly establishes that the HHI score of a less concentrated market&mdash;and not another noun in the sentence&mdash;is being compared with the HHI score of a highly concentrated market.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of \"a market that is less concentrated\" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that a market is being compared with an HHI score. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a confusing, illogical sentence. The placement of the modifier \"when a market is less concentrated\" after \"a highly concentrated market\" is contradictory. Furthermore, it&rsquo;s ambiguous what \"its\" is referring to in \"its HHI score,\" resulting in an illogical comparison. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a dangling modifier. The placement of \"a less concentrated market\" immediately after the modifying phrase illogically suggests that a market is being compared with an HHI score.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061193,"updateDate":1755117061193}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061193, 1755117061193),
    ($SATQ$fdb16e20$SATQ$, $SATQ$344d2279-223b-4e5e-89b6-609e08488125$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$d96900eb-43fa-40a2-8422-80a75cd4757e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Quantum particles of light&mdash;photons&mdash;provide an unhackable means of transmitting encryption keys over networks, as attempts to observe particles in quantum states will invariably alter the particles <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> dismantle any information they transmit.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"23930215-3148-48cb-a6a5-8c8f21981755","content":"<p><strong> </strong>and in the process,</p>"},{"id":"ba6eb520-5aed-4dfc-93a8-1f44d4170ac0","content":"<p><strong> </strong>and, in the process,</p>"},{"id":"2fb39aaa-57dd-4af4-80e0-3dba9c0235e0","content":"<p><strong> </strong>and in the process&mdash;</p>"},{"id":"80b8d738-320a-4ff4-9347-5f2b36f47085","content":"<p><strong> </strong>and, in the process</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["ba6eb520-5aed-4dfc-93a8-1f44d4170ac0"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. This choice uses paired punctuation in the form of two commas to set off the nonessential phrase "in the process."</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The phrase "in the process" is a nonessential element and needs to be set off with paired punctuation. We need a comma after "and" to match the one after "process." Choice C is incorrect. The phrase "in the process" is a nonessential element and needs to be set off with paired punctuation, so we would need a dash after "and" to match the one following "process." Choice D is incorrect. The phrase "in the process" is a nonessential element and needs to be set off with paired punctuation. We would need a comma after "process" to match the one following "and."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-BOU-H010$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$3d05bfdf-75ec-4c1c-9cbd-e0eaf9c2a6dc$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"fdb16e20","external_id":"344d2279-223b-4e5e-89b6-609e08488125","vaultid":"d96900eb-43fa-40a2-8422-80a75cd4757e","uId":"aec1d551-41b0-4bf3-b6f2-90b89459fb65","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Quantum particles of light&mdash;photons&mdash;provide an unhackable means of transmitting encryption keys over networks, as attempts to observe particles in quantum states will invariably alter the particles <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> dismantle any information they transmit.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"23930215-3148-48cb-a6a5-8c8f21981755","content":"<p><strong> </strong>and in the process,</p>"},{"id":"ba6eb520-5aed-4dfc-93a8-1f44d4170ac0","content":"<p><strong> </strong>and, in the process,</p>"},{"id":"2fb39aaa-57dd-4af4-80e0-3dba9c0235e0","content":"<p><strong> </strong>and in the process&mdash;</p>"},{"id":"80b8d738-320a-4ff4-9347-5f2b36f47085","content":"<p><strong> </strong>and, in the process</p>"}],"keys":["ba6eb520-5aed-4dfc-93a8-1f44d4170ac0"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. This choice uses paired punctuation in the form of two commas to set off the nonessential phrase \"in the process.\"</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The phrase \"in the process\" is a nonessential element and needs to be set off with paired punctuation. We need a comma after \"and\" to match the one after \"process.\" Choice C is incorrect. The phrase \"in the process\" is a nonessential element and needs to be set off with paired punctuation, so we would need a dash after \"and\" to match the one following \"process.\" Choice D is incorrect. The phrase \"in the process\" is a nonessential element and needs to be set off with paired punctuation. We would need a comma after \"process\" to match the one following \"and.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-BOU-H010","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"3d05bfdf-75ec-4c1c-9cbd-e0eaf9c2a6dc","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959359,"updateDate":1691007959359}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959359, 1691007959359),
    ($SATQ$fe41f258$SATQ$, $SATQ$f960db6c-ca24-4344-9627-af95fbefd793$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$f507d6d7-13ec-4f94-a8b8-13fcb6ab5b72$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In ancient Greece, an Epicurean was a follower of Epicurus, a philosopher whose beliefs revolved around the pursuit of pleasure. Epicurus defined pleasure as &ldquo;the absence of pain in the body and of trouble in the <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> that all life&rsquo;s virtues derived from this absence.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"6d3918c8-488c-4144-bd98-67fac5ec9a2f","content":"<p>soul,&rdquo; positing</p>"},{"id":"e3a8b357-708e-4cbf-987f-c0c2f58380ec","content":"<p>soul&rdquo;: positing&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"7d1dee9f-892b-459a-8c94-806a27fb276b","content":"<p>soul&rdquo;; positing</p>"},{"id":"2c7c0038-c652-4abb-966d-26ec2e8b4669","content":"<p>soul.&rdquo; Positing&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6d3918c8-488c-4144-bd98-67fac5ec9a2f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between a main clause and a participial phrase. This choice correctly uses a comma to mark the boundary between the main clause (&ldquo;Epicurus&hellip;&lsquo;soul&rsquo;&rdquo;) and the participial phrase (&ldquo;positing&hellip;absence&rdquo;) that provides additional information about how Epicurus defined pleasure. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect because a colon can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join a main clause and a participial phrase. Choice C is incorrect because a semicolon can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join a main clause and a participial phrase. Choice D is incorrect because it results in a 