    ($SATQ$03503d49$SATQ$, $SATQ$b56c5e06-9287-4cf4-860c-261d954a284d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$05716914-10ca-4fbe-a73e-bc0755142ad7$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Algebra$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, $SATQ$H.E.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Linear inequalities in one or two variables$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">A business owner plans to purchase the same model of chair for each of the <math alttext="81"><mn>81</mn>
</math> employees. The total budget to spend on these chairs is <math alttext="dollar sign 14,000"><mo>$</mo><mn>14,000</mn>
</math>, which includes a <math alttext="7 percent sign"><mn>7</mn>
<mo>%</mo></math> sales tax. Which of the following is closest to the maximum possible price per chair, before sales tax, the business owner could pay based on this budget?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"2bdf9eb6-d491-45bc-9cf3-3706a5345852","content":"<p><math alttext=\"dollar sign 148.15\"><mo>$</mo><mn>148.15</mn></math></p>"},{"id":"7fdfdfa6-87c3-457d-b385-286d64aa6320","content":"<p><math alttext=\"dollar sign 161.53\"><mo>$</mo><mn>161.53</mn></math></p>"},{"id":"151d7537-794a-4cf0-95e4-e4739f2813c1","content":"<p><math alttext=\"dollar sign 172.84\"><mo>$</mo><mn>172.84</mn></math></p>"},{"id":"f022684a-d5ea-451e-9efa-ba1edb1dbd66","content":"<p><math alttext=\"dollar sign 184.94\"><mo>$</mo><mn>184.94</mn></math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["7fdfdfa6-87c3-457d-b385-286d64aa6320"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is correct. It&rsquo;s given that a business owner plans to purchase <math alttext="81"><mn>81</mn>
</math> chairs. If <math alttext="p"><mi>p</mi>
</math> is the price per chair, the total price of purchasing <math alttext="81"><mn>81</mn>
</math> chairs is <math alttext="81 p"><mrow>
	<mn>81</mn>
	<mi>p</mi>
</mrow>
</math>. It&rsquo;s also given that <math alttext="7 percent sign"><mn>7</mn><mo>%</mo></math> sales tax is included, which is equivalent to <math alttext="81 p"><mrow>
	<mn>81</mn>
	<mi>p</mi>
</mrow>
</math> multiplied by <math alttext="1.07"><mn>1.07</mn>
</math>, or <math alttext="81 left parenthesis 1.07 right parenthesis p"><mn>81</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>1.07</mn><mo>)</mo><mi>p</mi></math>. Since the total budget is <math alttext="dollar sign 14,000"><mo>$</mo><mn>14,000</mn></math>, the inequality representing the situation is given by <math alttext="81 left parenthesis 1.07 right parenthesis p less than or equals 14,000"><mn>81</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>1.07</mn><mo>)</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>14,000</mn></math>. Dividing both sides of this inequality by <math alttext="81 left parenthesis 1.07 right parenthesis"><mn>81</mn><mo>(</mo><mn>1.07</mn><mo>)</mo></math> and rounding the result to two decimal places gives&nbsp; <math alttext="p less than or equals 161.53"><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>161.53</mn></math>. To not exceed the budget, the maximum possible price per chair is&nbsp;<math alttext="dollar sign 161.53"><mo>$</mo><mn>161.53</mn></math>.</p>
<p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>
<p>Choice C is incorrect. This is the maximum possible price per chair including sales tax, not the maximum possible price per chair before sales tax.</p>
<p>Choice D is incorrect. This is the maximum possible price if the sales tax is added to the total budget, not the maximum possible price per chair before sales tax.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$manifold$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"03503d49","external_id":"b56c5e06-9287-4cf4-860c-261d954a284d","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":"05716914-10ca-4fbe-a73e-bc0755142ad7","uId":"b3466f7c-a0c6-4812-8a19-2a325eadb507","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"H","domain":"Algebra","skill_cd":"H.E.","skill_desc":"Linear inequalities in one or two variables","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">A business owner plans to purchase the same model of chair for each of the <math alttext=\"81\"><mn>81</mn>\n</math> employees. The total budget to spend on these chairs is <math alttext=\"dollar sign 14,000\"><mo>$</mo><mn>14,000</mn>\n</math>, which includes a <math alttext=\"7 percent sign\"><mn>7</mn>\n<mo>%</mo></math> sales tax. Which of the following is closest to the maximum possible price per chair, before sales tax, the business owner could pay based on this budget?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"2bdf9eb6-d491-45bc-9cf3-3706a5345852","content":"<p><math alttext=\"dollar sign 148.15\"><mo>$</mo><mn>148.15</mn></math></p>"},{"id":"7fdfdfa6-87c3-457d-b385-286d64aa6320","content":"<p><math alttext=\"dollar sign 161.53\"><mo>$</mo><mn>161.53</mn></math></p>"},{"id":"151d7537-794a-4cf0-95e4-e4739f2813c1","content":"<p><math alttext=\"dollar sign 172.84\"><mo>$</mo><mn>172.84</mn></math></p>"},{"id":"f022684a-d5ea-451e-9efa-ba1edb1dbd66","content":"<p><math alttext=\"dollar sign 184.94\"><mo>$</mo><mn>184.94</mn></math></p>"}],"keys":["7fdfdfa6-87c3-457d-b385-286d64aa6320"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is correct. It&rsquo;s given that a business owner plans to purchase <math alttext=\"81\"><mn>81</mn>\n</m